// Reordering the toolbar was reachable only from a test until 2026-08-31 (issue I-43):
// `QuickAccessToolbar::moveCommand` existed, `UiCustomization` persisted the order, three
// documents called it shipped, and nothing on screen could move a button. Left and right rather
// than up and down because the toolbar is a row in both of its placements.
RAWPAGE_ACTION(UiQatLock,              "ui.qatLock",              Global, "action.ui.qatLock")
// The two move commands stay REGISTERED although the context menu no longer offers them: drag is
// the gesture, and a gesture reaches nothing but a pointer. The batch runner and a chord still
// need a way to reorder the toolbar -- see the standing rule about flow K in CLAUDE.md.
RAWPAGE_ACTION(UiQatMoveLeft,          "ui.qatMoveLeft",          Global, "action.ui.qatMoveLeft")
RAWPAGE_ACTION(UiQatMoveRight,         "ui.qatMoveRight",         Global, "action.ui.qatMoveRight")

// =============================================================================================
// ALIASES -- append only, never delete (rule 2 above, decision D1-16)
// =============================================================================================
// Each row keeps a renamed id resolving from a saved shortcut or QAT entry written by an older
// build. There are none yet: nothing has been renamed, because nothing has shipped. The first
// rename in a later stage adds the first row here IN THE SAME CHANGE as the rename -- that is the
// point at which this section stops being empty and starts being load-bearing.
//
// The `tst_actionregistry` alias fixture exercises the mechanism against a synthetic table rather
// than against this list, so the machinery is tested before the first real rename needs it, not
// after. A mechanism whose first use is also its first test is a mechanism nobody knows works.

// Both macros are undefined unconditionally, so an includer defines exactly the one it cares about
// and never has to clean up. The alternative -- leaving them defined -- makes the second include in
// a translation unit silently reuse the first one's expansion, which is the classic X-macro bug.
#undef RAWPAGE_ACTION
#undef RAWPAGE_ACTION_ALIAS
